{
  "abstract": "We reduce the ten-color question for biplanar line graphs of arbitrary finite loopless multigraphs to four explicit graphs on 28 vertices. The Goldberg–Seymour edge-coloring theorem and Szeider’s independence-two result imply that every biplanar line graph is ten-colorable, and any example requiring ten colors contains the line graph of an eight-regular multigraph on seven vertices. Clique and planar density constraints leave exactly 27 multigraph classes. A separate exhaustive integer verifier checks this census, covering 88,875 labeled matrices. Explicit triangle-free witnesses and separator inequalities exclude nineteen classes, tight-separator disk congruences exclude three more, and a face-incidence formula with independently audited faces and cuts and a checked DRAT refutation excludes one additional class. The four survivors have 150, 152, 153 and 154 edges. Literal ten-colorings and nine-colorings after every vertex deletion certify their chromatic and vertex-critical status. We supply their complete specifications and a portable verifier that repeats the census, exclusions, coloring checks, and final SAT proof. We also give a constructive weighted-cycle coloring proof that removes a longer-cycle caveat in the earlier clique-blow-up literature. The four remaining graphs have unresolved biplanarity; no ten-chromatic biplanar construction or unrestricted Earth–Moon resolution is claimed.",
